---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { site } from '../../data/site';
import { guides } from '../../data/guides';

const guide = guides.find((g) => g.slug === 'get-recommended-by-chatgpt')!;
const dateLabel = (d: string) => new Date(d).toLocaleDateString('en-IN', { day: 'numeric', month: 'long', year: 'numeric' });

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'Article',
    '@id': `${site.url}/guides/${guide.slug}#article`,
    headline: guide.title,
    description: guide.summary,
    image: `${site.url}/og-image.png`,
    datePublished: guide.published,
    dateModified: guide.updated,
    inLanguage: 'en-IN',
    mainEntityOfPage: `${site.url}/guides/${guide.slug}`,
    author: { '@id': `${site.url}/about#founder`, '@type': 'Person', name: site.founder, url: `${site.url}/about` },
    publisher: { '@id': `${site.url}/#org` },
    about: ['Generative engine optimisation', 'Answer engine optimisation', 'Google AI Overviews', 'Local SEO'],
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={guide.title}
  fullTitle="How to get your business recommended by ChatGPT | Nextmarg"
  description="An eight-step checklist for Indian businesses to show up in ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity and Google AI Overviews: test, open up, get listed, answer, get mentioned, track."
  crumbs={[{ name: 'Guides', path: '/guides' }]}
  crumbName="Get recommended by ChatGPT"
  schema={schema}
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="wrap" style="max-width:860px">
      <span class="kicker">Guide · AI search</span>
      <h1 class="display">{guide.title}</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        By <a href="/about">{site.founder}</a>, founder of Nextmarg · Published {dateLabel(guide.published)}
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <article class="wrap prose">
      <p class="lead" style="max-width:none">
        <strong>The short answer:</strong> AI tools like ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity and Google AI Overviews name the businesses they can find,
        understand and trust. Let their crawlers read your site, keep your business details identical everywhere, publish one clear page per
        service with prices and areas, answer real customer questions in plain words, and get other trusted sites and reviewers to mention you.
        Then test the same questions every month.
      </p>

      <h2>How AI tools decide which businesses to name</h2>
      <p>
        When someone asks "best physiotherapist in HSR Layout", most AI assistants now search the web before they answer. Google's AI
        Overviews and AI Mode draw on Google's index. Microsoft Copilot uses Bing. ChatGPT and Perplexity run their own web searches. Each
        then picks a few businesses that several sources agree on.
      </p>
      <p>That means three things matter:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Can they read you?</strong> Your site must be open to their crawlers and indexed by both Google and Bing.</li>
        <li><strong>Do they understand you?</strong> Your pages must say clearly what you do, where, for whom and at what price.</li>
        <li><strong>Do others vouch for you?</strong> Reviews, directories, news and industry lists that mention you by name.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Step 1: Test where you stand today</h2>
      <p>
        Write down 10 questions a customer would really ask, such as "best dentist near Indiranagar for kids" or "who sets up Zoho CRM in
        Bengaluru". Ask each one in ChatGPT, Gemini, Perplexity, Copilot and Google, using a private browser window. Note who gets named and
        which websites are linked as sources. Those source sites are where you need to appear.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 2: Let AI crawlers read your website</h2>
      <p>
        Open <code>yourwebsite.com/robots.txt</code>. If you see <code>Disallow: /</code> under GPTBot, OAI-SearchBot, PerplexityBot,
        ClaudeBot or Google-Extended, those tools can't read you. If your site uses Cloudflare, also check <em>Security → Bots</em>: newer
        Cloudflare accounts can block AI crawlers by default. Allow at least the search crawlers.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 3: Make your business details identical everywhere</h2>
      <p>
        Your name, phone number, address or service area and hours should match exactly on your website, Google Business Profile, Bing
        Places, Justdial, Practo or IndiaMART, and social profiles. Mismatched details make AI tools less sure you are one real business.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 4: One clear page for each service</h2>
      <p>
        A single "Services" page listing ten things is hard for AI to quote. Give each main service its own page that answers, in the first
        two sentences: what it is, who it's for, where you offer it, and a starting price. AI tools prefer pages that state facts plainly over
        pages that only say "contact us for details".
      </p>

      <h2>Step 5: Answer real questions, in your customers' words</h2>
      <p>
        Add a short FAQ to each service page using the questions customers ask you on the phone and WhatsApp. Start each answer with the
        answer itself, then the detail. Ask your web developer to add <em>schema markup</em> (structured data) for your business, services
        and FAQs, so machines read the same facts people do.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 6: Get mentioned on sites AI tools trust</h2>
      <p>
        This is the step most businesses skip, and the one that matters most for recommendations. Ask happy customers for Google reviews
        that mention the service and area. Get listed on the directories that appeared in your Step 1 sources. Get quoted in local news,
        association websites and "top 10" lists in your industry. Never buy fake reviews: Google removes them and can suspend your profile.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 7: Add your customers' language</h2>
      <p>
        If many of your customers think and search in Kannada, Hindi, Tamil or Telugu, add pages in that language, written naturally and
        checked by a native speaker. Very few local businesses have them, so it is one of the easiest ways to stand out in both Google and
        AI answers.
      </p>

      <h2>Step 8: Track the same questions every month</h2>
      <p>
        Repeat your Step 1 test on the same date each month and keep a simple sheet: question, tool, were you named, who was named instead.
        AI answers vary, so look at the trend over three months rather than one result. Pair it with Google Search Console and Bing Webmaster
        Tools, which are free and show the searches that already bring people to your site.
      </p>

      <h2>What doesn't work</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Anyone who guarantees you a place in ChatGPT. Nobody controls AI answers.</li>
        <li>Stuffing pages with keywords or city names. AI tools summarise meaning, not repetition.</li>
        <li>An <code>llms.txt</code> file on its own. It is a cheap, sensible extra, but no major AI tool has said it uses it for ranking.</li>
        <li>Fake or paid reviews. They break Google's rules and can get your listing suspended.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Want us to check for you?</h2>
      <p>
        Nextmarg's free <a href="/digital-health-score">Digital Health Score</a> includes an AI search check: we test real customer questions
        across the main AI tools and tell you your top five fixes. If you'd like us to do the work, see our
        <a href="/services/seo-ai-search">SEO and AI search services</a>.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">Last updated {dateLabel(guide.updated)}.</p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <CtaBand title="Does ChatGPT recommend you, or your competitor?" />
</Base>
